  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Post-Ebola Multisectorial response for the reduction of infections in Equateur</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Since  1 June 2020 (statement by the Provincial Minister of Health), Equateur has recorded the 11th outbreak of Ebola Virus Disease (EVD) in the Democratic Republic of Congo (DRC), which was brought under control five months after its outbreak. This was made possible by a multi -sectoral strategy to reduce the impact on individuals and communities while linking affected people to care institutions. This Ebola outbreak, which occurred during the COVID -19 pandemic, has made the humanitarian response context more complex.   As of 18 November 2020 (date of declaration of the end of the epidemic), statistics show 130 registered cases, including 11 probable cases and 53 deaths in 41 health areas of the 13 out of 18 affected health Zones.    In relation to its partners, including Oxfam, United Nations Children&apos;s Fund (UNICEF) intends to encourage community involvement and to limit the risks of propagation in the event of an outbreak of Maladie Virus Ebola (MVE), and to promote prevention and control activities against MVE, which remains a concern.  The province of Equateur faces constraints in terms of ICP(Infections, Control and Prevention ) -Water Sanitation and Hygiene (WASH) despite multiple interventions following this latest response to the EVM, they relate to the optimal functioning of the health system and the contingency plans are not systematic to the resurgence of other episodes of EVM.   A multisectoral post -ebola response is needed to reduce health care associated infections and strengthen community resilience against MVE and covid -19 in 18 Health Zones including Mbandaka, Wangata, Bolenge, Bikoro, Iboko, Ntondo, Ingende, Lotumbe, Monieka and Bolomba in Equateur Province.   In this framework of support to the Government in the coordination of the fight, UNICEF, through funds received from the World Bank on behalf of the Government of the DRC( Democratic Republic of Congo), has provided its own financial and human resources to ensure this fight against diseases a</narrative>
  </description>
